**What and why.** A factory centralises object creation behind an interface, so client code depends on an abstraction (`Notifier`) and not on concrete classes (`EmailNotifier`, `SmsNotifier`). Use it when the concrete type depends on runtime input or configuration, when construction is complex or needs shared setup, or when you want to swap implementations (and mock them in tests) without touching callers. Do not use it when there is one implementation and no variation; a plain constructor is simpler.

```java
interface Notifier { void send(String msg); }
class EmailNotifier implements Notifier { public void send(String m){ /* ... */ } }
class SmsNotifier implements Notifier { public void send(String m){ /* ... */ } }

class NotifierFactory {
    static Notifier create(String type) {
        switch (type) {
            case "email": return new EmailNotifier();
            case "sms":   return new SmsNotifier();
            default: throw new IllegalArgumentException(type);
        }
    }
}
```

**Open/Closed Principle.** Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification: add behaviour by adding code, not by editing tested code.

**Where the factory above violates it.** Adding a `PushNotifier` forces you to edit `create`, so the factory is not closed for modification. It also becomes a change hotspot and a merge-conflict magnet, and clients that use `instanceof` or type checks after creation break the Liskov Substitution Principle too.

**Resolutions.**
- A registry: `Map<String, Supplier<Notifier>>`; new types call `register("push", PushNotifier::new)` at startup, so the factory never changes.
- Plugin discovery (Java `ServiceLoader`, Spring beans collected by type) so implementations register themselves.
- Abstract Factory when you must create families of related objects.
- Dependency injection, so most callers receive a `Notifier` and never call the factory.

**Trade-offs.** A registry moves the failure to runtime (a missing key) and needs good error messages and tests; keep the key set explicit. Over-using factories adds indirection without value.
